          <a href="#nozzle" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Nozzle and Inlet Screen</a>
          <a href="#unloader" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Unloader Valve</a>
          <a href="#cycling" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. On/Off Cycling</a>
          <a href="#worn" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Worn Seals and Valves</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A pressure washer usually pulses because the pump is not getting enough water or is drawing air, so it surges between full pressure and almost none. The fix is most often a fully open faucet, a shorter or wider supply hose, a clean inlet screen and purging the air before you start; a partly blocked nozzle or a sticking unloader valve comes next. If instead your electric machine keeps switching itself on and off while the trigger is released, that is a different problem: the total stop system is sensing a pressure leak, usually at a fitting, the gun or an internal valve. Work through the checks in order, because the cheap causes are by far the common ones.
        </p>

        <div id="two-symptoms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is It Pulsing, or Is It Cycling On and Off?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before fixing anything, pin down which symptom you have, because people use the word pulsing for two different behaviors. The first is a spray that surges while you hold the trigger: strong, weak, strong, weak, often with the pump sound rising and falling and the hose twitching. The second happens on electric machines with a total stop system: you release the trigger, the motor stops as designed, then it restarts by itself for a second or two, stops again, and repeats every few seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surging during spraying is almost always about what goes into the pump. If water or air is not arriving steadily, the pump cannot deliver steady pressure. On/off cycling with the trigger released is usually about pressure leaking out of the system after the pump. The total stop switch sees pressure fall, assumes you have pulled the trigger, and starts the motor to rebuild it. Different symptoms, different ends of the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a third pattern worth knowing, which is normal. On many total stop machines, the motor starts with a brief stutter or surge the moment you squeeze the trigger, then settles. A second or so of that is expected while pressure builds. It only becomes a fault if it continues throughout the spray, or if the motor starts and stops repeatedly while you hold the trigger steady.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you see</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most likely cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Go to</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spray surges while trigger held</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water supply or air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Supply and air checks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surging plus higher-than-normal pressure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Partly blocked nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nozzle check</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Motor restarts every few seconds, trigger released</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leak downstream of pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">On/off cycling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surging persists with good supply and clean nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unloader or worn valves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unloader and worn parts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Motor cuts out completely after a while</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overheat or GFCI trip</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">On/off cycling</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If your complaint is weak pressure rather than pulsing, with a steady but feeble spray, most of the same checks apply, but our guide to a <a href="/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washer not building pressure</a> is organized around that symptom and covers worn nozzles and wrong tips in more depth. This page concentrates on the surge and the stop-start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="supply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is the Water Supply Too Weak?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most common cause by a long way. A pressure washer pump does not create water; it pressurizes whatever arrives at the inlet. If the machine wants, say, 1.5 gallons per minute and the supply delivers less, the pump runs short on every stroke. It sucks against the shortage, pulls air or vapor, and the output surges. That starving also causes cavitation, which erodes the valves and seals, so a pulsing machine should be fixed rather than worked through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the obvious. Open the outdoor faucet all the way, not just most of the way. Check the full length of the garden hose for kinks, especially near the faucet and where it bends behind the machine. Make sure nobody else in the house is running the shower, the washing machine or an irrigation system at the same time. Each of those steals flow from the same supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at the hose itself. A long, narrow garden hose loses a lot of flow, and many cheap hoses are only 1/2 inch inside diameter. A 5/8 inch hose of reasonable length is the common recommendation for pressure washers, and many manuals specify a maximum supply hose length. If you are running 100 feet of thin hose to reach the far side of the house, try a short, wide hose and move the machine instead, then use the high-pressure hose for reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check whether the supply is enough, time how long the hose takes to fill a 5-gallon bucket with the faucet fully open. Divide 5 by the minutes it took to get gallons per minute, and compare it with the GPM on your machine's label. If the supply is not comfortably above the machine's rating, you have found the cause. Homes on wells, with pressure-reducing valves or with old galvanized pipes are particularly prone to this. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washer water supply requirements</a> covers the bucket test and fixes in detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE BUCKET TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your supply should beat the label.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Fill a 5-gallon bucket with the faucet wide open and time it. If the flow you calculate is not comfortably above the machine's rated GPM, the pump is being starved and will surge.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Could There Be Air in the Line?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Air is the second most common cause, and it often follows the first. If you started the motor before water was flowing through the pump, or if the supply hose was disconnected and reconnected mid-job, the pump can trap air pockets that it struggles to push out against the nozzle. The result is a sputtering, surging spray, sometimes with a rattling pump sound, especially in the first minute or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Purging air is simple. Switch the machine off. Connect the supply hose, open the faucet fully, and squeeze the trigger with the motor still off until a steady, bubble-free stream comes out of the gun. On many machines that takes 30 seconds or more. Then switch on and spray with the widest nozzle for a short time before changing to a narrower tip. Removing the nozzle entirely while purging, if your gun allows it, speeds things up.
          </p>

          <p>
            If air keeps coming back, look for where it is getting in. A loose hose connection at the inlet, a cracked or missing washer in the garden hose coupling, or a damaged inlet fitting can all draw air on the suction side while barely leaking water. Replace the hose washers, check that the inlet screen is seated properly, and tighten connections by hand. A bit of air is enough to cause surging that seems out of proportion to the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a pressure washer from a tank or bucket is a special case. Many consumer machines are not designed to draw water up from a container and will pulse or fail to prime if you try. If your manual allows siphon or tank feeding, keep the suction hose submerged, short and free of leaks. If it does not, use a pressurized supply. Starting the pump without water is also covered in our guide on <a href="/how-long-can-a-pressure-washer-run-without-water/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long a pressure washer can run without water</a>, because the two problems go together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nozzle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Could a Nozzle or Inlet Screen Be Blocked?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A clogged inlet screen produces the same starving effect as a weak supply. Almost every pressure washer has a small mesh filter in the water inlet, and it gradually collects sand, rust flakes and debris from the hose. Unscrew the inlet fitting, pull out the screen, and rinse it under a tap. If it is torn or impossible to clean, replace it rather than running without it, because the grit it catches would otherwise go straight into the pump valves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A partly blocked spray nozzle causes a different kind of pulsing. With the outlet restricted, pressure rises above normal, the unloader starts diverting water, and the spray flutters between full and reduced. You may also notice the spray pattern is uneven or splits into separate jets. Clean the nozzle with the tip-cleaning tool that came with the machine, or a fine wire, then flush it backward with water. Do this with the machine off and the pressure released from the gun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try a different nozzle to confirm. If the pulsing stops with the wide white or green tip and returns with the narrow one, the narrow tip is probably partly blocked or damaged. If the pulsing happens with every nozzle, the nozzle is not the cause, and you can move on. Turbo nozzles and surface cleaners can also make the flow feel uneven by design, so test with a standard tip before blaming the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worn nozzles usually produce weak, steady pressure rather than pulsing. As a nozzle orifice erodes over time it gets larger, which lowers pressure. That belongs to the low-pressure family of faults, which our <a href="/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">not building pressure guide</a> covers. If your nozzles are several seasons old and heavily used, a cheap replacement set is worth having anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="unloader" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Could the Unloader Valve Be Sticking?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The unloader valve controls where water goes when you release the trigger. With the trigger pulled, water goes out through the hose and nozzle; when you let go, pressure spikes and the unloader shifts to divert water back around the pump, so the system is not left dead-headed. If the unloader is sticking, worn or contaminated, it can shift back and forth while you spray, which feels like pulsing even when the water supply and nozzle are fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            On consumer machines, the unloader is often built into the pump and is not designed for user adjustment. Some have a small screw or knob for pressure control, and turning it out of range can cause surging. If yours has an adjustment, return it to the manufacturer's default setting before anything else. On machines with no adjustment, the main things an owner can do are to flush debris out with clean water and check that the supply is clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Debris is the usual reason an unloader sticks. Grit that gets past a damaged inlet screen, mineral scale from hard water and residue from detergents not meant for pressure washers can all leave the valve sluggish. Running clean water through the machine for a few minutes after using detergent, and using pump saver products in storage, helps keep it moving freely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unloader replacement is a reasonable repair on many gas machines and on pressure washers with serviceable pumps, and parts are often available by model number. On budget electric units with sealed pumps, the unloader may be part of the pump assembly. If the supply, air and nozzle checks are clean and the machine still surges, it is time to compare the cost of the part with the cost of the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cycling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Does My Electric Pressure Washer Keep Turning On and Off?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most electric pressure washers have a total stop system, sometimes abbreviated TSS. When you release the trigger, pressure in the hose and gun rises, a pressure switch senses it, and the motor shuts off. When you squeeze the trigger again, pressure drops, the switch closes, and the motor starts. It is a sensible design that saves wear and prevents overheating in bypass. But it means any pressure leak downstream of the pump looks to the switch like you pulling the trigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the classic symptom is the motor switching back on every few seconds, or every few minutes, while you are not spraying. Something is letting pressure bleed off. The usual suspects are a leaking quick-connect at either end of the high-pressure hose, a cracked or missing O-ring, a leaking spray gun or trigger valve, a damaged hose, or a check valve inside the pump that is no longer sealing. Faster cycling usually means a bigger leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for drips while the machine is pressurized and off. Check each connection with the trigger released and the motor stopped, and feel for moisture around fittings and the gun. Replace the O-rings in the quick-connects, which are cheap and often the culprit, and tighten threaded connections. If there is no visible leak anywhere outside the machine, the leak may be internal, through a worn check valve or the unloader, which is a pump repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            A motor that cuts out completely while you are spraying is a different problem. Most electric machines have thermal overload protection that stops the motor when it gets too hot, and it may restart after cooling. Overheating is commonly caused by a long or undersized extension cord, high ambient heat or extended use. The GFCI in the power cord can also trip if moisture reaches a connection. Use the cord gauge and length the manual allows, keep connections dry, and let the machine cool.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cycling with the trigger released means a leak.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The total stop switch only restarts the motor when pressure falls. If it restarts on its own, pressure is escaping somewhere between the pump and the nozzle, often at an O-ring.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="worn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Could Worn Seals or Valves Be the Cause?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have a strong supply, no air, a clean inlet screen, a clean nozzle and a properly set unloader, and the machine still surges, internal wear is the likely cause. Inside the pump, small check valves let water into each piston chamber and out again. When one fails to seal, from wear, a stuck bit of debris or heat damage, that piston stops contributing on each stroke, and the output becomes uneven. The rhythm of the surge often matches the pump's rotation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Piston or plunger seals can cause the same thing. Worn seals let water bypass the piston, which reduces output and can make it uneven. Seal wear is often accompanied by water dripping from the pump body, especially under pressure. Running the machine dry, leaving it idling in bypass, feeding it hot water and long periods of cavitation from a weak supply all wear seals faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some pumps can be repaired. Check valve kits and seal kits are available for many gas pressure washer pumps and for triplex pumps, and replacing them is a reasonable job for a careful DIYer with the right parts. On many budget electric machines, the pump is a sealed unit and the practical repair is a complete replacement. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-do-electric-pressure-washers-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long electric pressure washers last</a> explains when a pump repair is worth it and when replacement makes more sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are replacing the machine, choose one with a metal pump head, available parts, and an induction motor if you use it often. Our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washer guide</a> compares those details. Whatever you buy, the habits that prevent pulsing are the same: a full, cool water supply, purging air before starting, clean screens and nozzles, and switching off rather than idling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working through the pulsing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulsing usually means the pump is starved and cavitating, which erodes valves and seals with every minute you continue. Finishing the driveway first can turn a supply problem into a pump problem. Stop, check the faucet and hose, purge the air, and only resume once the spray is steady.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the pump before checking the supply</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most pulsing is caused by something outside the machine: a partly open faucet, a kinked or narrow hose, another tap running in the house or a weak well supply. A five-minute bucket test costs nothing and resolves most cases. Pump repairs and replacements should be the last step, not the first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting the motor before purging air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the motor runs before water flows through the gun, the pump traps air and surges for the rest of the session. Connect the hose, open the faucet fully, and squeeze the trigger with the motor off until a steady, bubble-free stream comes out. Then switch on with the widest nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a cycling motor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A total stop machine that keeps restarting with the trigger released is telling you pressure is leaking. Leaving it plugged in and cycling wears the switch and motor and wastes water. Turn it off, find the leak, often a cheap quick-connect O-ring, and fix it before your next session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running without the inlet screen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Removing a clogged inlet filter instead of cleaning it lets sand and scale into the pump, where it lodges in check valves and the unloader and causes exactly the surging you were trying to fix. Rinse the screen, or replace it if it is torn. They cost very little.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it normal for a pressure washer to surge when I first pull the trigger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A brief surge or stutter at the moment you squeeze the trigger is normal on many electric machines with total stop, because the motor is starting and pressure is building. It should settle within a second or two. If the surging continues throughout the spray, or the motor stops and starts while you hold the trigger, there is a fault to diagnose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a garden hose cause pressure washer pulsing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the most common causes. A long hose, a narrow 1/2 inch hose, kinks, or a cracked hose washer can starve the pump or let air in. A shorter 5/8 inch hose, fully opened faucet and fresh washers fix many cases. Check the manual for the supply hose length and size it allows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my pressure washer pulse with one nozzle but not others?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That points to the nozzle. A partly blocked narrow tip restricts flow, raises pressure and makes the unloader cycle, which feels like pulsing. Clean the tip with the cleaning tool or a fine wire and back-flush it. If it still pulses, replace it. If every nozzle pulses, the problem is elsewhere in the system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a pulsing pressure washer damage itself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Pulsing from a starved supply usually means cavitation, where vapor bubbles collapse inside the pump and erode the valves and seals. The longer you work through it, the more damage builds. Stop and fix the supply or air problem before continuing, and the pump will usually recover without lasting harm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my electric pressure washer from turning on and off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Find the pressure leak. With the motor stopped and the trigger released, check the quick-connects at both ends of the high-pressure hose, the spray gun and the hose itself for drips. Replace worn O-rings and tighten connections. If nothing leaks externally, a worn check valve or unloader inside the pump is the likely cause and needs repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can low water pressure at the house cause pulsing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The pump needs a steady supply that meets or exceeds its rated gallons per minute. Homes on wells, with pressure-reducing valves, or with old pipes may not deliver enough flow, especially if other water is being used at the same time. Time a 5-gallon bucket fill to measure what your faucet actually delivers and compare it with the label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does detergent cause a pressure washer to pulse?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can contribute. Thick cleaners, or products not designed for pressure washers, can leave residue on the unloader and valves that makes them sluggish. Drawing detergent through a siphon tube that has a leak can also pull air into the system. Use pressure-washer detergents, and flush with clean water for a few minutes afterward.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

