        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A pressure washer that surges while you spray is nearly always short of water or full of air. Open the faucet fully, use a shorter, wider hose, run the bucket test, clean the inlet screen and purge the air before starting. If the pulsing follows one nozzle, clean or replace that nozzle. Only after those checks does the unloader or the pump itself become a suspect.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An electric machine that keeps switching itself on and off with the trigger released is a different fault: pressure is leaking somewhere after the pump, and the total stop switch is responding to it. Find the drip, often a worn O-ring, and the cycling stops. If the leak is inside the pump, or worn valves cause surging despite a good supply, weigh repair against replacement based on what the machine is worth.
          </p>
        </div>

